{"answer":"To determine compute requirements for large-scale suggesting systems, you can start from scaling-law style studies that relate model performance to computational investment and identify inefficiencies that prevent predictable scaling. For recommendation, Kunlun targets poor scaling efficiency and proposes architectural and optimization choices meant to improve model FLOPs utilization and resource allocation, yielding measurable gains in scaling efficiency that can guide compute budgeting. [2] Behavioral foundation model work similarly studies how compute-optimal recipes depend on architecture choices (like parameter split and batch/model/data allocation) and training negatives, showing that the “compute-optimal” configuration can shift with compute budget and even with the evaluation metric. [4] For reranking, scaling laws can be fitted to forecast larger reranker sizes and derive compute-allocation rules. [6]

For system-level compute estimation, you can combine scaling-law guidance with more granular profiling to attribute where time/compute goes inside heterogeneous recommendation architectures. Component Benchmark provides hierarchical, submodule-based profiling so you can characterize individual modules (memory-, compute-, and low-arithmetic-intensity ops) rather than only end-to-end throughput, supporting more accurate compute requirement planning for large deployments. [5] Additionally, ROCS shows how inference-time compute can be reduced by request-oriented compute sharing, implying compute requirements depend strongly on model execution strategy (e.g., sharing across candidates). [1]

These differ in scope: scaling laws focus on compute vs. quality predictability across model/training settings [2,4,6], while profiling and inference-efficient architectures address execution and deployment costs directly. [5,1]","grounded":true}
